VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DEI-012
Reason for Visit: Acute respiratory distress and severe dermatological flare-up requiring immediate medical intervention to stabilize airway and systemic inflammation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute exacerbation of coughing, audible wheezing, and spreading severe pruritic rash following an industrial emission event in the patient's neighborhood. Urgent face-to-face assessment was necessary to administer bronchodilators and rule out impending respiratory failure prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I can't stop coughing, my skin is burning, and the chemical smell outside is suffocating me tonight.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a multi-year history of disproportionate environmental exposure due to their residential zip code, manifesting as chronic, unexplained respiratory irritation and persistent low-grade systemic inflammation linked to proximity to industrial pollutants. Over the past 7 days, there has been a known increase in local industrial flaring. This evening, the patient developed acute, severe asthma-like symptoms, including relentless coughing, chest tightness, and a severe flare of contact dermatitis on exposed skin. The patient expresses elevated baseline anxiety concerning the safety of local water and air quality, chronic olfactory distress from persistent environmental odors, and feelings of profound systemic neglect by municipal health bodies.
Functional Status: Functionally impaired by acute respiratory distress and severe pruritus.
Social/Safety Concerns: Ongoing, unmitigated exposure to environmental toxins due to socioeconomic inability to relocate.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 138/85 | HR: 110 | Temp: 37.0 | RR: 24 | SpO2: 94% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Patient is sitting upright, visibly tachypneic, frequently coughing, and aggressively scratching their forearms. A distinct industrial chemical odor is perceptible upon entering the residence.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Tachycardia, regular rhythm, no murmurs. Respiratory: Diffuse, bilateral expiratory wheeze with a prolonged expiratory phase. Mild intercostal retractions noted. Dermatological: Extensive, excoriated, erythematous plaques on the bilateral forearms, neck, and face, consistent with severe contact dermatitis or atopic flare. Neurological: Alert and oriented, but highly anxious regarding the air safety. Water/air safety anxiety is objectively validated by the immediate environmental conditions (industrial odor, poor air quality index locally).
Environmental Observations: Windows are taped shut, and several commercial air purifiers are running at maximum capacity in the small living space.
Medication Reconciliation: Salbutamol inhaler used 6 times in the last 2 hours with minimal relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Environmental Exposome Disparity (Environmental Racism) - Psych. Factors Affecting Other Medical Conditions (F54) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Anaphylaxis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of upper airway involvement (no stridor, tongue swelling, or lip angioedema) and normal blood pressure.

Congestive Heart Failure - Ruled out/unlikely due to the prominent expiratory wheeze without bibasilar crackles, normal jugular venous pressure, and no peripheral edema.

Panic Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary cause; while anxiety is present, objective physical signs of bronchospasm (wheeze, SpO2 94%) and dermatological inflammation confirm a primary organic response to environmental triggers.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, symptomatic bronchospasm (RR 24, SpO2 94%, bilateral wheeze) and an aggressive dermatological flare directly tied to disproportionate environmental toxic exposure. Immediate, out-of-hours face-to-face assessment was critical to deliver nebulized bronchodilator therapy, assess for impending respiratory exhaustion, and prescribe high-potency topical corticosteroids. Delaying assessment until the morning clinic hours posed a severe risk of acute hypoxic respiratory failure overnight, strictly satisfying the criteria for an urgent after-hours medical intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered nebulized salbutamol 5mg and ipratropium bromide 500mcg on site, yielding improvement in SpO2 to 97% and reduction in wheeze. Prescribed oral prednisolone 37.5mg daily for 5 days to reduce systemic inflammation. Recommended usual GP check blood lead levels, urinary concentrations of volatile organic compound metabolites, and systemic inflammatory markers.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage the chronic respiratory irritation safely, implement a strict "inhaler tracking protocol": use the preventer inhaler twice daily, brushing teeth immediately afterward to prevent thrush, and log it on a mirror chart; this visual reinforcement builds adherence to the preventative regimen. To manage the dermatological flare, apply emollient heavily immediately after a lukewarm, 3-minute shower; trapping the moisture will restore the skin barrier and actively reduce the localized burning sensation.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to an environmental health advocacy clinic.
DME/Safety (Optional): Continued use of HEPA air purifiers.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 24 hours to monitor respiratory status and taper oral steroids.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or contact 000 if experiencing an inability to speak in full sentences, blue lips, severe drowsiness, or if the inhaler provides relief for less than 2 hours.

